          <article class="archive-item" id="post-18344">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-10-02">2 October 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>Why CISOs Struggle to Answer the Board&#x27;s Three Hardest Questions, and How to Fix the Report</h3>
            <p>Security leaders struggle to confidently answer board questions about organizational security exposure and financial risk due to fragmented data across multiple tools.</p>
            <p>The quarterly board meeting is two weeks away. The security team is pulling exports from the identity provider, the cloud posture tool, the vulnerability scanner, the SIEM and the EDR console. Someone is building a spreadsheet to reconcile them. Someone else is turning that spreadsheet into slides.</p>
            <p>A board member asks three questions. How secure is the organization, overall? What is the actual financial exposure? Is the security posture better than it was last quarter? Most security leaders cannot answer any of them with confidence. Not because the data doesn’t exist, but because it lives in a dozen tools that don’t share context.</p>
            <p>Boards want exposure, not activity. Which business-critical assets could an attacker actually reach today? They want trend, not snapshot. And they want money, not CVEs. The real problem lives in the gaps between tools. Each tool is accurate about its own slice. None of them sees how the slices connect.</p>
            <p>A contractor account in the identity provider still holds a group membership from a finished project. That group grants access to a SaaS app with an OAuth integration into the cloud environment. The integration runs under a service account with broad storage permissions. That storage holds customer records. No single dashboard shows it, so it doesn’t make the board report. It gets found during an incident instead.</p>
            <p>The CISO’s guide to confident board reporting takes on this problem. It shows how to rebuild the report around exposure. Define the crown jewels with the business. Connect what is already deployed. Map real attack paths. Prioritize by blast radius. Translate exposure into financial terms. Report the trend.</p>
          </article>
