            <p>Malicious Custom GPTs are using trusted domains to deliver RATs through fake ChatGPT interfaces by tricking users into executing PowerShell commands.</p>
            <p>Malicious Custom GPTs are exploiting trusted domains to deliver RATs through ClickFix-style attacks. Attackers mimic legitimate ChatGPT instances to trick users into executing PowerShell commands that download malware. This leverages the trust users have in OpenAI and Google infrastructure to bypass traditional defenses. The infection chain starts with a fake CAPTCHA and ends with a RAT that can steal data and map networks.</p>
            <p>This attack highlights the growing threat of prompt injection and the need for stronger AI governance. Users are being lured by seemingly official interfaces, making it critical to question what any AI interface is asking them to do. Training should focus on identifying suspicious prompts rather than just verifying sources. The line between legitimate and malicious AI is increasingly blurred.</p>
