            <h3>AI Agents Are Privileged Users; Who Is Auditing Their Access?</h3>
            <p>AI agents are being treated as privileged users with access to sensitive systems but lack proper auditing and controls to prevent potential security risks.</p>
            <p>AI agents are becoming the next generation of insider threats. We rigorously audit human employees, but often overlook the broad privileges granted to autonomous AI agents. These agents operate as non-human identities with access to sensitive systems and data. They can orchestrate actions across cloud repositories, databases, and corporate environments, yet many security teams treat them as benign software integrations.</p>
            <p>This blind spot creates a serious risk. An agent executing code, provisioning resources, or modifying financial records behaves like a privileged user in every practical sense. Traditional access controls fail to address this because agents often use long-lived tokens and bypass human-in-the-loop checks. The result could be a production outage, data integrity issue, or unbudgeted cloud spend spike.</p>
            <p>To prevent this, we must treat AI agents as privileged identities. Security architectures need to move agentic AI into the scope of identity and access management. Before granting production access, we must pressure-test the architecture against operational realities. Agents should have isolated, non-interactive identities with short-lived tokens. Their access must be strictly scoped to their business purpose.</p>
            <p>Governance, audit, and control frameworks are essential. We need telemetry that captures prompt inputs, tool calls, and downstream API executions. Regular access certification reviews and containment mechanisms are also critical. If we don&#x27;t audit and govern AI agents with the same discipline we apply to human users, threat actors may exploit them as automated insider threats.</p>
          </article>
